{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And let me fetch a morsel of bread.</b> The righteous say little and do much, for afterward he brought them a tender and good calf." "<b>And sustain your hearts.</b> From here one may learn the meaning of סמך and סעד — that he recognized in them the nature of angels, even though they came to him in the likeness of men. Therefore he said לבכם and did not say לבבכם, in the manner of what is written concerning flesh and blood (Psalms 104:15): ״ולחם לבב אנוש יסעד״ — for flesh and blood has two hearts (לבבות), a good impulse and an evil impulse; but the angels have but one heart, toward their Father in heaven. Thus from the missing letter bet we learn many matters. So too (Exodus 31:17): ״כי ששת ימים עשה ה' את השמים ואת הארץ״ — which by way of the plain sense (peshat) should have said בששת; and the missing letter bet teaches us the days of the world, for the Holy One, blessed be He, made the heavens and the earth six days — that is, six thousand, for a day of the Holy One, blessed be He, is a thousand years, as it says (Psalms 90:4): ״כי אלף שנים בעיניך כיום״ and so forth. And from this you can understand that not a single letter in the Torah is superfluous, but all is in wisdom and in complete providence. Thus Moses, peace be upon him, testified to us at the end of the Torah (Deuteronomy 32:46–47): ״כי לא דבר רק הוא מכם״, and our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: if it is ריק (empty) from you, it is רק (empty). And what is written of flesh and blood (Psalms 48:14): ״שיתו לבכם לחילה״, and it does not say לבבכם — is it not speaking of the future, when there will be no evil impulse there? Therefore our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: לחולה כתיב — it is written 'to the sick' — and that is the known מחול (sickness) for the righteous in the world to come." "<b>Since you have therefore passed by.</b> Therefore I say 'a morsel of bread,' a small thing, because you passed by your servant suddenly — had I known, I would have prepared a great feast:" "<b>And they said: Do so as you have spoken.</b> By way of moral instruction: a morsel of bread is enough — do not trouble yourself with other things. And it is possible to interpret כן תעשה כאשר דברת — 'do so as you have spoken' — to mean: you shall take the morsel of bread, but you shall not eat of it. This hints to him regarding the future, in what happened at the end with the cakes of Sarah, which she had kneaded — for that reason he did not bring them before them with the calf:"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {1 "The citation in the Hebrew for Psalms 104:15 is written as תהילים ק״ד:ט״ו, which is correct. For Psalms 48:14 the Hebrew reads תהילים מ״ח:י״ד. The play on רק/ריק draws on rabbinic interpretation of Deuteronomy 32:47. The final phrase לחולה כתיב (b. Bava Batra 16b, etc.) identifies 'sick' (חולה) with מחול, alluding to the righteous who will be healed of the evil impulse in the world to come."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:13:13.515100684Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}